WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.313 --> 00:00:06.544
[Antani] I went to school for computer engineering in India.

00:00:13.815 --> 00:00:18.539
I've worked with image processing, computer vision, pattern recognition, machine learning.

00:00:18.539 --> 00:00:25.011
So, my world was filled with developing algorithms that could extract interesting

00:00:25.011 --> 00:00:28.224
objects from images&nbsp;and videos.

00:00:33.534 --> 00:00:38.350
Pattern Recognition is a family of techniques that looks for particular

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:43.546
pixel characteristics or voxel characteristics inside an image and learns to recognize those&nbsp;objects.

00:00:48.147 --> 00:00:54.255
Deep Learning is a way of capturing the knowledge inside an image and&nbsp;
encapsulating it

00:00:55.623 --> 00:01:01.633
and then researchers like me spend time advancing&nbsp;
newer deep learning networks

00:01:01.633 --> 00:01:04.754
that look more broadly into an image,

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:13.225
recognizing&nbsp;
these objects, recognizing organs in my case,

00:01:13.225 --> 00:01:21.227
and diseases, and converting those visuals&nbsp;
into numerical risk predictors that could be used by clinicians.

00:01:21.227 --> 00:01:27.890
So, my research is&nbsp;
currently in three very different areas. One area looks at cervical cancer.

00:01:30.904 --> 00:01:35.364
A machine could look at the images and be a&nbsp;
very solid predictor of 

00:01:35.364 --> 00:01:39.738
the risk to the woman of developing cervical pre-cancer encouraging early&nbsp;treatment.

00:01:42.848 --> 00:01:47.661
Another area I work with, Sickle Cell Disease. One of the risk factors in Sickle Cell Disease is

00:01:47.661 --> 00:01:52.465
Cardiac Myopathy or&nbsp;
Cardiac Muscle Disease, which leads to stroke and perhaps even death.

00:01:56.141 --> 00:02:03.034
Looking at cardiac echo videos and using AI to&nbsp;
be a solid predictor, along with other blood lab tests,

00:02:04.868 --> 00:02:07.000
improves the chances of survival.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:12.896
A third area that I'm interested in is understanding the&nbsp;
expression of Tuberculosis

00:02:15.761 --> 00:02:17.232
 in chest x-rays,

00:02:18.783 --> 00:02:25.384
particularly for children and those that are HIV positive. The&nbsp;expression of disease in that sub-population

00:02:25.384 --> 00:02:29.453
is very different from adults with TB who are not&nbsp;
HIV positive.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:34.804
Every clinician have seen a certain number&nbsp;
of patients in their clinical training.

00:02:36.492 --> 00:02:42.964
They perhaps have spent more&nbsp;
time at hospitals or clinical centers, and exposed to certain population,

00:02:42.964 --> 00:02:45.819
and they become very adept&nbsp;
at that population.

00:02:45.819 --> 00:02:50.147
Machines, on the other hand, could be trained on data that is free of&nbsp;bias,

00:02:51.691 --> 00:02:56.328
from different parts of the world, different&nbsp;
ethnicities, different age groups, so that there's

00:02:56.328 --> 00:03:00.431
an improved care giving and therefore a&nbsp;
better expectation on treatment and care.

